# Non-classical Logics

* Motivation

- @Problem@: classical logic (propositional, first-order) assumes a complete,
  static world where conclusions never change once proven
  - Real-world knowledge is incomplete, exception-ridden, and evolves as new
    facts arrive
  - E.g., _"birds fly"_ holds by default, but breaks once you learn about
    penguins

- @Key idea@: relax classical logic along two axes
  - _Ontological commitment_: what exists in the world
  - _Epistemological commitment_: what an agent believes about facts

- @Techniques@: this lecture covers
  - Non-monotonic and default reasoning, common sense reasoning, open vs
    closed world assumption
  - Description logics (ALC, SHOIN) and the Web Ontology Language (OWL)
  - Knowledge representation standards: RDF, SPARQL
  - Knowledge graphs and semantic networks (WordNet, ConceptNet, WikiData,
    DBpedia)

* Ontological Commitment

- @Definition@: **Ontological commitments** are assumptions about reality made by
  a language `#cite("russell2020aima")`{=typst}
  - Different formal models make different assumptions on how truth is defined

::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}

- @Examples@:
  - _Propositional logic_: world consists of facts that are either true or false
    - E.g., $P \lor Q$
  - _First-order logic_: world consists of objects with relations that hold or do
    not hold
    - E.g., $\forall x: Human(x) \implies Mortal(x)$
  - _Higher-order logic_: relations can be objects themselves
    - E.g., assertions about relations like _"all relations are transitive"_
  - _Temporal logic_: facts hold at particular times or intervals
    - E.g., _"rain occurred at time t"_

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

```graphviz
digraph OntologicalLevels {
    rankdir=TB;
    node [shape=box, style=filled, fontname=Helvetica];

    root [label="Logical Systems", fillcolor="#C6A6F4"];

    prop [label="Propositional Logic\n(facts: true/false)", fillcolor="#A0D6D1"];
    fol [label="First-Order Logic\n(objects & relations)", fillcolor="#A0D6D1"];
    hol [label="Higher-Order Logic\n(relations as objects)", fillcolor="#A0D6D1"];
    temp [label="Temporal Logic\n(facts at times)", fillcolor="#A0D6D1"];

    root -> prop;
    root -> fol;
    root -> hol;
    root -> temp;
}
```

::::
:::

// TODO(ai_gp): Add temporal logic example

* Epistemological Commitment

- @Definition@: **Epistemological commitment** is the possible states of
  knowledge an agent holds with respect to each fact

- @Examples@
  - _Propositional_, _first-order logic_: 3 possible states of belief (true, false, unknown)
  - _Probability theory_: each sentence has a degree of belief in [0, 1]
    - E.g., $\Pr(X = 6) = 0.3$

## Non-monotonic Reasoning and Common Sense

* Non-monotonic Logic

::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}

- @Definition@: **Non-monotonic logic** is a logic where adding new information
  can invalidate previous conclusions `#cite("mccarthy1980circumscription")`{=typst}

- @Comparison@:
  - _Classical logic_: once proven, conclusions remain true even with new
    information
  - _Non-monotonic logic_: conclusions change as new facts are learned

- @Intuition@:
  - Real-world reasoning involves incomplete or evolving knowledge
  - Non-monotonic logic allows systems to reason flexibly and adapt to new
    circumstances

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

```graphviz
digraph NonMonotonicReasoning {
    rankdir=TB;
    node [shape=box, style=filled, fontname=Helvetica];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica];

    kb1 [label="Initial KB:\nBirds typically fly\nTweety is a bird", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    conc1 [label="Conclusion:\nTweety can fly", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];

    newFact [label="New Fact:\nTweety is a penguin\nPenguins cannot fly", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];

    kb2 [label="Updated KB", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    conc2 [label="Revised Conclusion:\nTweety CANNOT fly", fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];

    kb1 -> conc1 [label="reasoning"];
    conc1 -> newFact [label="conflict"];
    newFact -> kb2 [label="update"];
    kb2 -> conc2 [label="revise"];
}
```

::::
:::

- @Example@
  - Initial KB: _"Birds typically fly"_, _"Tweety is a bird"_
  - Conclusion: _"Tweety can fly"_
  - New fact: _"Tweety is a penguin"_ and _"penguins cannot fly"_
  - Revised conclusion: _"Tweety cannot fly"_

* Default Reasoning

- @Definition@: **Default reasoning** makes assumptions by default in the absence
  of contrary evidence, allowing conclusions based on typical situations unless
  exceptions are found `#cite("reiter1980default")`{=typst}

- @Key idea@:
  - Assume the most likely case unless specified otherwise
  - If new information contradicts the assumption, revise the conclusion

- @Example@
  - Default rule: _"Typically, birds can fly"_
  - Fact: _"Tweety is a bird"_
  - Conclusion: _"Tweety can fly"_
  - New fact: _"Tweety is a penguin"_
  - Revised: _"Tweety cannot fly"_

- @Pros@: allows systems to function reasonably without complete information

* Non-Monotonic Logic: University Example

- **Initial facts**
  - $Alice$ is a $Student$
  - $Alice$ belongs to the $ComputerScience$ department
  - $CS101$ is a $Course$ offered by the $ComputerScience$ department

- **Default rule**:
  - Each $Student$ in the $ComputerScience$ department takes all classes in their
    department

- **Initial reasoning**
  - Since $Alice$ is a $Student$ in $ComputerScience$, by default $Students$
    take $CS101$
  - Conclusion: $takesCourse(Alice, CS101)$

- **New information**
  - $Alice$ is a student who does not meet the prerequisites for $CS101$

- **Revised reasoning**
  - New conclusion: $\lnot takesCourse(Alice, CS101)$

* Common Sense Reasoning

- @Problem@: Typical knowledge is often implicit and taken by granted by humans
  - _"If you drop a glass, it will likely break"_
  - _"People eat food when hungry"_

- @Definition@: **Common sense reasoning** makes assumptions and draws
  conclusions based on everyday knowledge about the world

- @Characteristics@
  - Deals with incomplete, uncertain, ambiguous information
  - Relies on defaults, heuristics, and typical patterns, not strict proofs
  - Flexible and tolerant of exceptions

- @Challenges@
  - Knowledge is vast, informal, imprecisely defined
  - Difficult to encode in machine-readable form
  - Handling exceptions and contradictions is complex

- @Techniques@
  - Knowledge graphs, non-monotonic logic, probabilistic reasoning, machine learning

* Common Sense Reasoning: Restaurant Example

::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}

- **Initial facts**
  - $Bob$ enters a $Restaurant$
  - $Bob$ sits at a $Table$

- **Common sense knowledge**
  - $Customers$ who sit $\implies$ intend to order
  - $Waiters$ bring $Menu$ before taking order
  - $Customers$ pay before leaving

- **Reasoning steps**
  - $Bob$ sat at table $\implies$ intends to eat
  - Therefore, $Bob$ will get menu and order

- **New information**
  - $Bob$ asks for directions only
  - Revise: $Bob$ will NOT order

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

```graphviz
digraph RestaurantExample {
    rankdir=TB;
    node [shape=box, style=filled, fontname=Helvetica];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=10];

    bob_enters [label="Bob enters\nRestaurant", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    bob_sits [label="Bob sits at\nTable", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];

    common_sense [label="Common Sense:\nSit at table\n→ intends to eat", fillcolor="#C6A6F4"];

    infer1 [label="Infer: Bob\nintends to eat", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];
    menu [label="Server brings\nMenu", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];
    order [label="Bob places\nOrder", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];

    new_info [label="NEW: Bob asks\nfor directions\nonly", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    revise [label="REVISE:\nNo order!", fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];

    bob_enters -> bob_sits;
    bob_sits -> common_sense [label="apply"];
    common_sense -> infer1 [label="conclude"];
    infer1 -> menu;
    menu -> order;

    order -> new_info [label="contradicts"];
    new_info -> revise [label="revise"];
}
```

::::
:::

* Open World vs Closed World Assumptions

\begingroup \small

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Aspect", "Closed World Assumption (CWA)", "Open World Assumption (OWA)"),
  rows: (
    ("Missing info", "False by default", "Unknown (not false)"),
    ("Example: Bob takes CS101?", "FALSE (not stated $\\implies$ false)", "UNKNOWN (not stated $\\implies$ unknown)"),
    ("DB systems", "Relational DB (SQL), logic programs", "Semantic Web (RDF, OWL)"),
    ("Best for", "Complete, static knowledge", "Incomplete, evolving data"),
    ("Query \"Bob takes CS101\"", "Returns false", "Returns no result (unknown)"),
  ),
  caption: "Closed World vs Open World Assumptions",
)
```

\endgroup

- @Example CWA@:
  - Fact: _"Alice takes CS101"_ is in the database
  - Nothing said about Bob
  - Conclusion: _"Bob does not take CS101"_ (assumed false)

- @Example OWA@:
  - Fact: _"Alice takes CS101"_ is known
  - Nothing said about Bob
  - Conclusion: _"Bob's enrollment is unknown"_ (could be true or false)

* Inductive Logic Programming

::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}

- @Definition@: **Inductive logic programming** learns logical rules from
  examples and background knowledge

- @Example@
  - Background: _"Birds have wings"_
  - Positive: _"Tweety is a bird that can fly"_
  - Negative: _"Penguin cannot fly"_
  - Learned: _"Birds fly unless penguins"_

- @Pros@:
  - Produces human-readable rules
  - Integrates learning with reasoning
  - Supports background knowledge

- @Challenges@:
  - Complexity with large datasets
  - Cannot handle noisy data

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

```graphviz
digraph ILP {
    rankdir=TB;
    node [shape=box, style=filled, fontname=Helvetica];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica];

    background [label="Background Knowledge:\nBirds have wings\nPenguins are birds", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];

    pos_ex [label="Positive Examples:\nTweety (bird) flies\nParrot (bird) flies", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];
    neg_ex [label="Negative Examples:\nPenguin cannot fly\nOstrich cannot fly", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];

    hypothesis [label="Hypothesis Space", fillcolor="#A0D6D1"];

    learned_rule [label="Learned Rules:\nBird(X) ∧ ¬Penguin(X)\n→ CanFly(X)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

    background -> hypothesis;
    pos_ex -> hypothesis;
    neg_ex -> hypothesis;
    hypothesis -> learned_rule;
}
```

::::
:::

# Knowledge Representation Frameworks

## Description Logics

// https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Description_logic

* Description Logic

- @Definition@: **Description logic** represents structured knowledge about a
  domain `#cite("baader2003dlhandbook")`{=typst}
  - Balances expressivity and computational efficiency
  - More expressive than propositional logic, less than first-order logic

::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}

- @Components@
  - _Classes_: abstract groups (e.g., $Person$, $Animal$)
  - _Properties_: binary relations (e.g., $hasChild$, $ownsPet$)
  - _Instances_: specific objects (e.g., $GP$, $Nuvolo$)

- **Reasoning tasks**
  - Concept subsumption: _"Is $A$ a subset of $B$?"_
  - Instance checking: _"Does $a$ belong to $A$?"_

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

```graphviz
digraph DescriptionLogic {
    rankdir=TB;
    node [shape=box, style=filled, fontname=Helvetica];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica];

    person [label="Class: Person", fillcolor="#C6A6F4"];
    pet_owner [label="PetOwner\n(Person with pet)", fillcolor="#A0D6D1"];
    gp [label="Instance: GP\n(a PetOwner)", fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];
    nuvolo [label="Instance: Nuvolo\n(GP's dog)", fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];

    person -> pet_owner [label="subclass"];
    pet_owner -> gp [label="instance"];
    gp -> nuvolo [label="ownsPet"];

    syntax_note [label="Syntax:\nPetOwner ≡ Person ⊓ ∃ownsPet.Dog", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", shape=note];
}
```

::::
:::

- **Syntax**: combines atomic concepts and roles with logical constructors
  ($\sqcap$, $\sqcup$, $\neg$, $\forall$, $\exists$)
  - E.g., $Father \equiv Man \sqcap \exists hasChild.Person$

- @Examples@
  - Used widely in ontologies (e.g., OWL)

* ALC

- @Definition@: **Attributive Concept Language with Complements (ALC)** is a
  basic but expressive description logic

- Combines logical operators
  - $\sqcap$ (and), $\sqcup$ (or), $\neg$ (not)
  - Existential and universal quantification: $\exists R.C$, $\forall R.C$
  - Set-theoretic interpretation (e.g., classes as sets, properties as binary
    relations)

- @Example@
  - _"All students take some course"_: $\text{Student} \equiv \exists \text{takes}.\text{Course}$
  - _"A mother is a woman with at least one child"_: $\text{Mother} \equiv \text{Woman} \sqcap \exists \text{hasChild}.\top$

- @Properties@
  - Decidable
  - Balances expressiveness and computational complexity
  - Foundation for more complex logics in OWL

* SHOIN

- @Definition@: **SHOIN** is a description logic more expressive than ALC
  `#cite("horrocks2003owl")`{=typst}

::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}

- @Components@:
  - $\mathcal{S}$: transitive properties (e.g., $ancestorOf$)
  - $\mathcal{H}$: role hierarchies (e.g., $hasSon \sqsubseteq hasChild$)
  - $\mathcal{O}$: specific individuals (e.g., $John$ nominal)
  - $\mathcal{I}$: inverse roles (e.g., $isChildOf$ inverse $hasChild$)
  - $\mathcal{N}$: cardinality constraints (e.g., _"exactly 1"_)

- @Example@: _"exactly two children"_: $Person \sqsubseteq (=2\ hasChild.\top)$

- @Pros@
  - More powerful, but harder reasoning
  - Models richer real-world scenarios
  - Foundation for OWL DL

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

```graphviz
digraph SHOINFeatures {
    rankdir=LR;
    node [shape=box, style=filled, fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=9];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica, fontsize=8];

    alc [label="ALC\n(base)", fillcolor="#A0D6D1"];

    s_node [label="S:\nTransitive\nProps\n(ancestorOf)", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    h_node [label="H:\nRole\nHierarchies\n(hasSon⊆hasChild)", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    o_node [label="O:\nNominals\n(John:individual)", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    i_node [label="I:\nInverse\nRoles\n(isChildOf)", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    n_node [label="N:\nCardinality\n(=2 children)", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];

    alc -> s_node;
    s_node -> h_node;
    h_node -> o_node;
    o_node -> i_node;
    i_node -> n_node;

    shoin [label="SHOIN\n(full)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    n_node -> shoin;
}
```

::::
:::

// https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_Ontology_Language

* OWL

::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}

- @Definition@: **Web Ontology Language (OWL)** `#cite("mcguinness2004owl")`{=typst}
  - Semantic web language for complex knowledge
  - Rich representation on the web (based on SHOIN)
  - Formal semantics for machine reasoning

- @Components@: classes, properties, individuals, axioms

- @Example@: _"Every cat is a mammal"_: $Cat \sqsubseteq Mammal$

- @Applications@: semantic search, biomedical data

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

```graphviz
digraph OWLVariants {
    rankdir=TB;
    node [shape=box, style=filled, fontname=Helvetica];
    edge [fontname=Helvetica];

    owl [label="Web Ontology\nLanguage (OWL)", fillcolor="#C6A6F4"];

    lite [label="OWL Lite\n(Simpler)\nfor classification", fillcolor="#A0D6D1"];
    dl [label="OWL DL\n(Full expressiveness)\nDecidable reasoning", fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];
    full [label="OWL Full\n(Maximum)\nUndecidable", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];

    owl -> lite;
    owl -> dl;
    owl -> full;

    lite -> dl [style=invis];
    dl -> full [style=invis];

    note1 [label="Lite:\nHierarchies only", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", shape=note, fontsize=8];
    note2 [label="DL:\nDecidable\n= practical", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", shape=note, fontsize=8];
    note3 [label="Full:\nTuring\ncomplete", fillcolor="#F4A6A6", shape=note, fontsize=8];
}
```

::::
:::

- @Variants@:
  - _OWL Lite_: simpler, for classification hierarchies
  - _OWL DL_: full expressiveness with decidable reasoning
  - _OWL Full_: maximum expressiveness, undecidable

* Example of OWL in RDF

::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}

```graphviz
digraph OWL_Example {
    rankdir=TD;
    node [shape=ellipse, style=filled, fillcolor=lightgray];

    Person [label="Person (Class)"];
    Student [label="Student (Class)"];
    hasAdvisor [label="hasAdvisor (ObjectProperty)", shape=box, fillcolor=lightblue];
    Restriction [label="Restriction: minCardinality 1", shape=diamond, fillcolor=lightyellow];

    Student -> Person [label="subClassOf"];
    Student -> Restriction [label="subClassOf"];
    Restriction -> hasAdvisor [label="onProperty"];
}
```

